        "original_id": "03bf52c0-b404-5c8f-88c4-01c9131737a7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Australian cricket team in England in 1981, 1981 Ashes series\n- People: Ian Botham\n- Topics: Botham's Ashes, Test cricket\n\nFacts:\n- The Australian cricket team toured England in 1981, and the tour included the 51st Ashes series of Test matches between Australia and England.\n- In the 1981 Ashes series, England were 1–0 down after two Tests but won the next three Tests and finished as 3–1 victors with two draws, thereby retaining the Ashes.\n- The 1981 Ashes series is popularly known as Botham's Ashes because of Ian Botham's remarkable performances with both bat and ball.\n- Sir Ian Terence Botham, born on 24 November 1955, is an English former first-class cricketer who was active from 1974 to 1993.\n- Ian Botham played mainly for Somerset and also for Worcestershire, Durham, and Queensland.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "47189677-a077-5054-b09e-8756ace45e8a",
        "text": "Page: Australian cricket team in England in 1981. Sentence: The tour by the Australian cricket team in England in 1981 included the 51st Ashes series of Test matches between Australia and England.\nPage: Australian cricket team in England in 1981. Sentence: Despite having been 1–0 down after two Tests, England won the next three Tests to finish 3–1 victors (with two draws), thus retaining the Ashes.\nPage: Australian cricket team in England in 1981. Sentence: The series is popularly known as Botham's Ashes, owing to the remarkable performances of Ian Botham with both bat and ball.\nPage: Ian Botham. Sentence: Sir Ian Terence Botham, OBE (born 24 November 1955) is an English former first-class cricketer, active 1974–1993, who played mainly for Somerset and also for Worcestershire, Durham and Queensland.",
